    "# Robustness Calculation\n",
    "\n",
    "## Definition from IEEE CAI 2026 Paper\n",
    "\n",
    "The robustness of a sample x with respect to a classifier C is defined as:\n",
    "\n",
    "$$r(C, x) = 1 - \\frac{\\max_{\\text{ICF} \\in AR_{C, y}} \\text{cost}_x(\\text{ICF})}{|\\text{features}|}$$\n",
    "\n",
    "Where:\n",
    "- $C$ is the classifier (e.g., Random Forest)\n",
    "- $x$ is the sample to evaluate\n",
    "- $y = C(x)$ is the classification of the sample\n",
    "- $AR_{C, y}$ is the set of **Anti Reasons** for class $y$\n",
    "- $\\text{cost}_x(\\text{ICF})$ is the cost function applied to sample $x$ for a given ICF\n",
    "- $|\\text{features}|$ is the total number of features in the dataset\n",
    "\n",
    "### Interpretation\n",
    "\n",
    "Robustness is a normalized value in the interval [0, 1]:\n",
    "\n",
    "- **r(C, x) close to 0**: Classification is UNSTABLE (small perturbations change classification)\n",
    "- **r(C, x) close to 1**: Classification is STABLE (large perturbations needed to change classification)\n",
    "- **r(C, x) = 0.5**: MODERATE stability threshold\n",
    "\n",
    "For Anti Reasons, low robustness indicates the perturbed region is easily reachable (high risk)."
   ],
